WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.190
Service path. Changes that come to you.

2
00:00:10.328 --> 00:00:13.018
What does the line ask of
me when upstream moves?

3
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.050
Your own change reaches main through a
pull request and a code owner's approval.

4
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.370
Upstream's changes come the other way.

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.680
Once a day, sync opens a pull
request into fork_upstream,

6
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.290
and a tracking issue, both
labeled human-required.

7
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.180
Human-required means it's your move:

8
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.710
review it and merge it.

9
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:37.630
Within seconds, Cascade
Monitor starts the cascade,

10
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.220
and the issue's labels move.

11
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.910
cascade-active while it
runs, then validated.

12
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.450
Cascade builds the change
with the fork's own code,

13
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.330
and opens a second pull request, to main.

14
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.810
This one gets the full lane:

15
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.170
an image, deployed and tested in a stack.

16
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.970
A person approves it and merges
it, and that closes the issue.

17
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.070
When the cascade can't
finish, the labels say so.

18
00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:10.510
Here, upstream moved to Java 25,
and the cascade's build failed.

19
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.580
The issue went cascade-blocked,
and a second issue opened,

20
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.820
labeled human-required.

21
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.540
The fix went to main as
an ordinary pull request,

22
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.540
in the provider's own pom.

23
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.110
Then a person ran the cascade again.

24
00:01:26.576 --> 00:01:28.336
That fix needed no label:

25
00:01:28.336 --> 00:01:30.506
the fork owns that path.

26
00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:34.736
Files that come from
fork_upstream are upstream-owned.

27
00:01:34.736 --> 00:01:38.906
If your pull request to main
changes one, Check Paths fails,

28
00:01:38.976 --> 00:01:40.906
and the Validation Summary with it.

29
00:01:41.216 --> 00:01:45.306
Here, a Dependabot pull
request touched the root pom.

30
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.070
To bring an upstream fix
in early, on purpose,

31
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.270
add the port label,
and re-run Check Paths.

32
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.870
It passes, and names the
file as a declared port.

33
00:01:59.096 --> 00:02:01.186
The third change is the line's own:

34
00:02:01.336 --> 00:02:04.546
the workflows every fork
gets from the template.

35
00:02:05.176 --> 00:02:10.376
Once a day, template sync proposes the
template's changes as one pull request to

36
00:02:10.376 --> 00:02:12.866
main, labeled template-sync.

37
00:02:13.416 --> 00:02:17.826
If the template moves again first,
it updates that same pull request.

38
00:02:18.508 --> 00:02:23.478
Its branch carries the template's
whole copy of each file that changed.

39
00:02:23.548 --> 00:02:26.598
Like the others, it waits
for a person to merge it.

40
00:02:28.608 --> 00:02:29.858
An easy mistake:

41
00:02:29.928 --> 00:02:33.778
template sync will overwrite the
workflow change I made in my fork.

42
00:02:33.848 --> 00:02:35.618
It opens a pull request.

43
00:02:35.928 --> 00:02:40.258
A local edit you want to keep
shows there as a change to review,

44
00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:43.938
and nothing lands on main
until a person merges.

45
00:02:44.248 --> 00:02:48.978
Check with gh pr list, and
the template-sync label.

46
00:02:51.628 --> 00:02:52.908
So now you can say:

47
00:02:52.908 --> 00:02:56.908
when upstream moves, the line
opens the pull requests,

48
00:02:56.908 --> 00:02:59.638
and its labels tell me when it's my move.

49
00:03:02.068 --> 00:03:03.838
Next, a deep dive:

50
00:03:03.908 --> 00:03:05.918
how is fork_upstream generated?

